<glossentry
    xml:id="papj-term-requirements-management"
    xml:lang="en-GB"
    xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook"
    version="5.1">
  <glossterm>Requirements management</glossterm>
  <glossdef>
    <simpara>
      The systematic process of eliciting, decomposing, documenting, prioritising, and tracing user and stakeholder requirements throughout the product lifecycle.
    </simpara>
    <simpara>
      Because users and <glossterm linkend="papj-term-stakeholder">stakeholders</glossterm> care about functionality and value, requirements should reflect this — leaving design, <glossterm linkend="papj-term-architecture">architecture</glossterm>, and technical choices to <glossterm linkend="papj-term-user-experience-design" baseform="User experience design">UX</glossterm> designers and <glossterm linkend="papj-term-technical-specification" baseform="Technical specification">specification</glossterm> engineers.
      In practice, however, these teams are often handed ‘requirements’ that already embed pre-design or pre-specification decisions.
    </simpara>
    <glossseealso otherterm="papj-term-formal-engineering">Formal engineering</glossseealso>
    <glossseealso otherterm="papj-term-integrated-engineering-environment">Integrated engineering environment</glossseealso>
    <glossseealso otherterm="papj-term-product-management">Product management</glossseealso>
    <glossseealso otherterm="papj-term-stakeholder">Stakeholder</glossseealso>
    <glossseealso otherterm="papj-term-technical-specification">Technical specification</glossseealso>
    <glossseealso otherterm="papj-term-user-experience-design">User experience design</glossseealso>
    <glossseealso otherterm="papj-term-validation">Validation</glossseealso>
  </glossdef>
</glossentry>
